WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.070 --> 00:00:03.639
Film Courage: What is sci-fi and what does
it mean to the two of you?

00:00:03.639 --> 00:00:04.639
Marc Scott Zicree: Wow!

00:00:04.639 --> 00:00:05.639
That’s an interesting question.

00:00:05.639 --> 00:00:06.639
That is a big question.

00:00:06.639 --> 00:00:10.960
Well…should I take it Elaine because I’m
the science fiction geek [laughs].

00:00:10.960 --> 00:00:17.550
Well science fiction [sighs] is a wonderful
playground because it allows us to comment

00:00:17.550 --> 00:00:20.182
on our world in a way that makes it much more
universal.

00:00:20.182 --> 00:00:27.779
For instance, when Rod Serling came up with
TWILIGHT ZONE he was censored by the networks

00:00:27.779 --> 00:00:32.189
and sponsors by saying anything he wanted
to say about politics or race or social issues

00:00:32.189 --> 00:00:33.189
of the day.

00:00:33.189 --> 00:00:37.590
So he created TWILIGHT ZONE so he could comment
on those things without the censors removing

00:00:37.590 --> 00:00:43.290
it and as a result those TWILIGHT ZONE episodes
are as timely now in the Trump era as they

00:00:43.290 --> 00:00:48.170
ever were when he was writing when Eisenhower
or Kennedy was president.

00:00:48.170 --> 00:00:55.060
So I love the fact that science fiction allows
me to reach an audience of people around the

00:00:55.060 --> 00:00:59.110
world and the work lasts decades and actually
centuries ultimately.

00:00:59.110 --> 00:01:04.820
Elaine Zicree: And it also seems to inspire
people because there are so many things that

00:01:04.820 --> 00:01:10.159
were jumped off from things like Star Trek,
where geek kids grew up to be geek scientists

00:01:10.159 --> 00:01:17.310
and they actually jumped in and they made
it real, so it really is inspirational.

00:01:17.310 --> 00:01:21.700
One thing I really like about science fiction
is it’s the same thing with stories in small

00:01:21.700 --> 00:01:22.910
towns or westerns.

00:01:22.910 --> 00:01:27.270
It’s where a lot of the complexities of
modern life is stripped away and we can really

00:01:27.270 --> 00:01:32.009
focus on moral and emotional issues in a much
clearer way.

00:01:32.009 --> 00:01:36.630
Marc Scott Zicree: Yes…And also if you kill
a character you can bring them back because

00:01:36.630 --> 00:01:37.630
you can clone them or whatever.

00:01:37.630 --> 00:01:38.630
Elaine Zicree: I’m particularly good at
that.

00:01:38.630 --> 00:01:41.860
Marc Scott Zicree: …Have their head in a
tank with nutrients or whatever.

00:01:41.860 --> 00:01:42.920
It’s really fun.

00:01:42.920 --> 00:01:49.190
Film Courage: Do you remember when the two
of you were exposed to the first sci-fi story

00:01:49.190 --> 00:01:52.670
and where you were, how old you were? Marc
Scott Zicree: Well between the two of us I’m

00:01:52.670 --> 00:01:53.750
the real science fiction geek.

00:01:53.750 --> 00:01:56.320
I mean Elaine was reading Dostoevsky when
she was a kid and Tolstoy…

00:01:56.320 --> 00:01:57.479
Elaine Zicree: But I wanted a robot!

00:01:57.479 --> 00:01:59.799
Marc Scott Zicree: You wanted a robot toy,
exactly.

00:01:59.799 --> 00:02:05.200
But my first experience with science fiction
when I was a kid I was reading comic books

00:02:05.200 --> 00:02:06.880
from as early as I can remember.

00:02:06.880 --> 00:02:12.510
My first favorite book was Farmer in the Sky
by Robert A. Heinlein… when I was age 7.

00:02:12.510 --> 00:02:16.260
I just grew up in the genres of TWILIGHT,
STAR TREK, THE OUTER LIMITS.

00:02:16.260 --> 00:02:18.740
Those were TV shows that made me want to be
a writer.

00:02:18.740 --> 00:02:23.380
And as soon as I was a teenager I ended up
going to science fiction conventions meeting

00:02:23.380 --> 00:02:27.400
those writers, the writers that had written
for those shows, Harlan Ellison, D.C.

00:02:27.400 --> 00:02:31.570
Fontana, David Gerrold, Theodore Sturgeon,
Richard Matheson, George Clayton Johnson,

00:02:31.570 --> 00:02:35.780
Ray Bradbury… and many of them became friends
and mentors.

00:02:35.780 --> 00:02:38.290
So I definitely knew where home was for me.

00:02:38.290 --> 00:02:43.520
Elaine Zicree: And I was more brought up on
some of the social issues of some of the older

00:02:43.520 --> 00:02:49.600
writers like 1984 [author George Orwell] and
H.G. Wells where they were exploring social

00:02:49.600 --> 00:02:50.600
issues.

00:02:50.600 --> 00:02:54.120
So rather than the newer writers, I knew the
older ones.

00:02:54.120 --> 00:02:59.680
My parents leaned strongly to the left and
so did I and we found their ability to explore

00:02:59.680 --> 00:03:03.780
things, where people would not put up walls
because it was in the future, allowed them

00:03:03.780 --> 00:03:08.420
a much greater freedom to reach into people’s
thought processes and feelings without defensiveness.

00:03:08.420 --> 00:03:14.900
Marc Scott Zicree: That’s sort of the sum
of it.

00:03:14.900 --> 00:03:19.230
When I was a kid I was given a Christmas present
of a trip to the STAR TREK set, so I was there

00:03:19.230 --> 00:03:22.940
for the last episode of the original STAR
TREK ever shot, Turnabout Intruder.

00:03:22.940 --> 00:03:25.620
So I got to sit in the captain’s chair and
stand on the transporter.

00:03:25.620 --> 00:03:29.310
I remember looking up and seeing there was
just a lightbulb screwed into the transporter

00:03:29.310 --> 00:03:31.830
above my head and I thought “Huh?

00:03:31.830 --> 00:03:32.830
Well, gee…”

00:03:32.830 --> 00:03:37.310
I remember thinking how small the engineering
on the STAR TREK set was a forced perspective

00:03:37.310 --> 00:03:40.340
and I was thinking “Gee, this is a lot smaller
than it looks on television.”

00:03:40.340 --> 00:03:45.680
And it was just an amazing day, a wonderful,
wonderful day.

00:03:45.680 --> 00:03:46.680
The best Christmas present ever.

00:03:46.680 --> 00:03:46.860
Question for the Viewers: What does Sci-Fi
mean to you?

